---
title: Converting an old iMac into mini PC and monitor
description: In my attempt to reduce my personal e-waste, I repurpose a beloved 27" iMac into an HDMI display and separate small form factor PC.
date: 2026-08-30
tags: [hardware, old tech]
---

My first personal computer was a Core 2 Duo MacBook from
Apple's "Black and White" era. It remains, in my opinion,
the best designed Apple laptop of all time, in the pantheon
of design greats with the [iMac G4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IMac_G4)
and [iPhone 4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone_4).

![Black Apple MacBook](./macbook.webp "Not my photo, as I didn't even have a cell phone at the time!")

Alas, the years took their toll on both the software and the physical case
(the black plastic had a tendency to crack along edges), and in early 2012
I had declared my Computer Science undergraduate major and was in need of a better device.

I knew I wanted a Mac, and I was willing to sacrifice portability for the beauty that was the
2011 iMac 27" display. With my student discount, I was able to pick up a top-of-the-line i7
from the refurbished store. It had barely come out of the box when I had already cracked it
open to swap in a faster SSD and upgrade the memory.

![iMac with the case removed](./insides.webp "Just thinking about removing the screen makes me nervous to this day")

I loved this machine. For a birthday, I even got a soft rolling case for it so I could bring it with me
back and forth to school, for internships in other states, to friends' dorm rooms to play League.

When I got an internship in Japan in 2014, I had no room for my iMac and had to purchase a MacBook, but even so,
the iMac remained my primary device until around 2020.

Around 2016, a latent graphics driver or hardware issue required a persistent DVI connection or it would instantaneously reboot,
which I worked around with a DVI cable and a dummy HDMI display emulator. When the internal hard drive died, I
shifted all important files to the SSD. Since Apple removed [Target Display Mode](https://support.apple.com/en-us/105126),
the use case for the machine became more and more limited.

Since 2020, it has lived in my parents' house, and got intermittent use for lightweight tasks like web browsing.

And then in 2025, it finally died completely and would not power on.

Perhaps it was sentimentality, or a consciousness of how much e-waste would be generated, or just a lingering affection
for the beautiful display, but this was not the ending I wanted.

So it was time to chop it up. The plan was simple: gut it for whatever internal components could be salvaged and get the display working
on its own.

The first part was easy. After removing the display, I separated components one at a time.

![A pile of internal computer components](./parts.webp)

iMacs are roughly a laptop platform, so the following parts could probably find use:

- cpu
- memory
- laptop-size graphics card
- drives

![The salvageable components](./salvage.webp)

Unfortunately a lot of other components would not be usable.
The motherboard is bespoke for the shape of the device, the power supply was likely broken,
and all of the form-molded internal components like fans and speakers were unlikely to be reused.

![Motherboard and heatsinks](./motherboard.webp)

![Internal fans and other plastic components](./fans.webp)

Assessing the parts, they were pretty standard LGA1155 components. A standard i7-2600, standard DDR3 SODIMMs, standard drives, etc etc.
With some converters, a power supply, and a CPU fan, it should fit nicely on a standard LGA1155 motherboard, so I grabbed one from eBay and
the rest of the parts from Newegg and set up a test bench.

I went with an Intel DH67GD, which had the right size (Micro ATX) and IO for my needs. It was ~$70 on eBay.
I also ordered some slightly sketchy SODIMM-DIMM converters, which are almost certainly not built to last, but what the hell.

![CPU and memory installed in the new motherboard](./memory.webp)

![Motherboard fully connected](./test.webp)

I wasn't sure if the SSD could still be trusted and with SSD prices at record highs, I was lucky to find
an old Samsung drive salvaged from yet another old Mac.

![Fully assembled computer inside](./assembled.webp)


With a quick flash of Ubuntu 24.04, I had managed to salvage the CPU, memory, and SSD, with around $140 in other components (case, power supply, motherboard).
This is probably a net loss, but it was fun and my conscience felt better about the reuse.


The real win would come from the display.


It's fairly easy to find controllers, and I found one for my screen variant `LM270WQ1(SD)(E3)` [on eBay](https://www.ebay.com/itm/155443699116) with an HDMI port.

![Display controller kit](./controllerkit.webp)

There were only two cables to attach, and with the guts of the iMac removed, it was easy to fit inside the cavity and wire out the back.
I preattached the HDMI cable and routed the other end out the back along with the power cable and controller.

![Cables coming out the iMac's back power hole](./cables.webp)

The end result was a little ugly, but I didn't feel like messing with the aluminum enclosure or drilling additional holes or anything like that, so it would suffice.

![Functioning iMac display](./display.webp)

While the internal components were probably net-negative on cost, for about $45 I was able to keep this beautiful 1440p display out of a landfill.

And just in time for my MacBook screen to crack!
